today on campaign check we examine  Labour's claim that rising Bills have  cost families thousands of pounds each  year since 2010 and their plans to  reverse them Labour say conservative LED  governments have cost the average  household almost six thousand pounds in  a decade but do their numbers add up one  of the largest increased cost labour  identify is rail season ticket they say  that by 2020 the average price will have  risen by one thousand seven hundred and  forty pounds for a household with two  regular rail commuters but they don't  take account of inflation that's  important because it identifies the real  impact of changing prices over the years  we put Labour's numbers through the Bank  of England inflation calculator and the  real terms increase in fares is 331  pounds for each ticket that's less than  half the Labour figure labour also said  the cost of childcare has gone up by an  average of almost two thousand pounds a  child since 2010 but these figures also  don't include inflation or the 3.7  billion pounds the government already  spends on free childcare so the real  figure will be significantly lower next  energy Labour say average bills have  risen by 68 pounds but again add  inflation in real terms bills have  fallen by 221 pounds the same goes for  water bills which Labour say have gone  up by 75 pounds but in real terms they'd  fallen by 13 on housing Labor's say the  median annual rent is increased by  almost 2,000 pounds in 10 years but at  inflation and in real terms  rents have fallen by 181 pounds labour  are closer on broadband they see bills  have risen 204 pounds in a decade though  adjusted for inflation it's about a  hundred and fifty two pounds now  Labour's say should they win power  they'll save the same household over  6700 pounds but this is not an average  family 5,000 pounds of Labour's savings  rely on rail fare and childcare savings  that are not available to the majority  of households only 5% of people use a  train more than three times a week which  means it will be highly unusual for a  family to have to season ticket-holder  while Labour's childcare calculation as  we've seen is unreliable and it would  only help those with a child under 4  anyway Labor's say there is a  cost-of-living crisis and while it is  true that wages have been stagnant in  real terms for 10 years this highly  selective analysis is flawed and does  not put a credible figure on the cost to  families
 

